{"topicSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/AllCliques","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvents","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/ClusteringCoefficients","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/CoreNumbers","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/OnionLayers","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TreeDecomposition","doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TrussNumbers"],"title":"Structures","generated":true,"anchor":"Structures"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvent"],"title":"Enumerations","generated":true,"anchor":"Enumerations"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/GraphProtocols"],"title":"Extended Modules","generated":true,"anchor":"Extended-Modules"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[[]]},"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/cliques"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Cliques"}],"roleHeading":"Framework","title":"Cliques","role":"collection","symbolKind":"module","externalID":"Cliques"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"references":{"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/AllCliques":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph whose size is in a range, once each, lazily,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order (by vertex index). The order is by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"type":"text","text":", then depth first, each clique followed by its extensions by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"later vertices in ascending index. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating again gives the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d) bitset words per clique for degeneracy d."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/AllCliques","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/allcliques","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"AllCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"AllCliques","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"AllCliques","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/CoreNumbers":{"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The core decomposition of an undirected graph (Batagelj–Zaversnik), over its simple graph. A","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex’s core number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every vertex has at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least k neighbors.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/CoreNumbers","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/corenumbers","title":"CoreNumbers","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"MaximalCliques","abstract":[{"text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"degeneracy d.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/GraphProtocols":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/graphprotocols","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/GraphProtocols","abstract":[],"role":"collection","title":"GraphProtocols","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/BronKerboschEvents":{"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/bronkerboschevents","type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"BronKerboschEvents"}],"abstract":[{"text":"The Bron–Kerbosch algorithm with pivoting as a sequence of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"BronKerboschEvent"},{"text":"s: the search of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maximalCliques()"},{"text":", one clique at a time. Pulling an event past the last one kept runs the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"search to its next maximal clique and keeps the events on the way.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"BronKerboschEvents"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvents","title":"BronKerboschEvents","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques","role":"collection","kind":"symbol","abstract":[],"title":"Cliques"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/ClusteringCoefficients":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ClusteringCoefficients"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Triangles and clustering for every vertex of an undirected graph’s simple graph, from one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pass."}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/clusteringcoefficients","type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ClusteringCoefficients"}],"title":"ClusteringCoefficients","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/ClusteringCoefficients","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/TrussNumbers":{"type":"topic","title":"TrussNumbers","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TrussNumbers","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/trussnumbers","abstract":[{"text":"The truss decomposition of an undirected graph (Cohen; Wang–Cheng), over its simple graph. An","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge’s truss number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every edge is in at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least k − 2 triangles: 2 for an edge in no triangle, k for an edge of a k-clique. Parallel","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edges share their simple edge’s number, and a self-loop has none.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TrussNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TrussNumbers","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/BronKerboschEvent":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvent","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/bronkerboschevent","fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BronKerboschEvent","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"What the Bron–Kerbosch algorithm with pivoting reports, in order, as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maximalCliques()"},{"type":"text","text":" runs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"it."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","title":"BronKerboschEvent","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"BronKerboschEvent","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/OnionLayers":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"OnionLayers"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/OnionLayers","title":"OnionLayers","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"OnionLayers"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The onion decomposition of an undirected graph’s simple graph: each vertex’s layer, a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"refinement of the core decomposition in which every layer lies inside one k-shell. Layers are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"numbered from 1; isolated vertices, when there are any, are layer 1 alone."}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/onionlayers"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/TreeDecomposition":{"title":"TreeDecomposition","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/treedecomposition","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A tree decomposition of a graph: bags of vertices, joined by edges into a tree, such that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"every vertex is in a bag, both ends of every edge are together in a bag, and the bags holding"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"any one vertex form a connected subtree."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TreeDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TreeDecomposition","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TreeDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}]}}}